//- 2:30 REDHEADS: the words drift over time too, and orange is barely a word, which hands straight to brown. docs/TALK.md, Claims, has the dates.
//- Two birth dates: the fruit's word in English (the Middle English Dictionary's c. 1380) and the color's ("orenge colour", 1502).
section.over-media(data-background-image="img/oranges.jpg" data-timing="10")
  h2 Orange.
  p The fruit: ~1380.#[br]The color: ~1502.
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Say it: orange is named after the fruit. English named the fruit a century before the color.
      li Evidence: the words drift over time too.
      li The fruit is in English by about 1380. The first "orenge colour" is in Elizabeth of York's wardrobe accounts in 1502.
      li Before that, red stretched to cover it.

//- The photo sits in the slide so the eyedropper lines up on any screen: pixel (228, 358) of img/hendricks.jpg is #cc8039, and at the portrait's 0.696 scale that lands at 62.4cqw, 34.6cqh.
section.over-media.portrait(data-background-color="#000" data-timing="15")
  img(src="img/hendricks.jpg" alt="Christina Hendricks, with copper hair, on a black background")
  +eyedropper(62.4, 34.6, "#cc8039")(class="fragment" data-fragment-index="1")
  h2 That's why redheads are red.
  cite.credit Photo: Romina Espinosa
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Arrive: everyone in this room would call that hair red.
      li Click: the eyedropper. #cc8039. Hue 29 degrees. That's orange.
      li Red for hair is late Old English, and "redhead" goes back to 1256. Red stretched to cover it, and the label stuck. So are robin redbreasts: they're fossils.
      li Never say red fox or red squirrel: both were named after orange existed.
      li Don't mention she dyes it. That's the next slide's punchline.

//- The payoff: the same woman, blonde, with the DLE's rojo in the corner on the click. The card quotes only the phrase we have from the entry, inside an ellipsis. Check the entry and set its exact wording before the night.
section.dictionary(data-background-image="img/hendricks-blonde-2.webp" data-timing="15")
  .entry.fragment(data-fragment-index="1")
    p.headword(lang="es") rojo
    p.sense(lang="es") «… de un rubio muy vivo»
    p.gloss
      | of hair: a very bright 
      mark blond
  cite.credit Photo: Marie Claire
  aside.notes
    ul
      li Say it: Spanish files them under red too. A redhead is a pelirrojo, a pelirroja. The robin is a petirrojo.
      li Never call a redhead "un rojo" or "una roja". In Spain that's a leftie, a commie, and it's loaded.
      li Click: and when you look up rojo in the RAE's dictionary, the sense for hair says: a very bright blond.
      li Christina Hendricks is a natural blonde. She's dyed it red since she was about ten. The RAE was right.
      li If she says why, it's "she says": Anne of Green Gables in one interview, Ann-Margret and Lucille Ball in another.
      li Don't say Spanish lacked the word. It had naranjado by 1351. Petirrojo is peto (bib) plus rojo, not pecho.
      li Check the DLE's entry for rojo before saying the blond line. It came from a search snippet, and the RAE site wouldn't load to check it.
